NOTICE: OpenBSD-derived files

Files under sys/ that are ports of OpenBSD sources carry their original
copyright notice and license at the top of the file, between the
<LICENSES> and </LICENSES> markers. That license governs that file. The
licenses of ported files are the ones OpenBSD accepted into its own tree:
ISC, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, BSD-4-Clause (its advertising clause
included), MIT, the Carnegie Mellon (Mach) license, Poul-Henning Kamp's
beerware license, John S. Dyson's license, the zlib license, permissive
notices in the HPND style (the IPsec authors, Carnegie Mellon, M.I.T., the
Open Software Foundation, Julian Elischer / TRW Financial Systems, David
Greenman's notice on the DP8390 driver), Intel's
EFI notice (the UEFI headers of sys/stand/efi/include, from FreeBSD), and
public domain. Files without license text keep their copyright lines as
they are. Provenance
per file is recorded in ports.toml; the upstream commit is recorded in
reference/PINNED.md. The names of OpenBSD contributors may not be used to
endorse or promote this project without their written permission
(BSD-3-Clause, clause 3).

Every Rust file under sys/ and tools/ also carries, first in its <LICENSES>
zone and before any original notices (or alone, where the source had none), an ISC
notice for the Rust work: Copyright (c) 2026 Emilio Navarrete Lineros
<enavarre@outlook.com>. The original notices are never altered; for a ported
file, both notices apply.

Files ported from zlib (Copyright (C) 1995-2026 Jean-loup Gailly and Mark
Adler) are altered source versions: they are rewritten in Rust and are not
the original software (zlib license, clause 2). Their license text is kept
in full in each file.

The ramdisk's userland is compiled unmodified from the OpenBSD sources and
keeps their licenses. Its tcpdump and libpcap include headers (bootp.h,
ppp.h) under a Carnegie Mellon notice: copying and distribution is by
permission of Carnegie Mellon and Stanford University.
